Description: Find a rekordbox track whose name the mount cannot spell
 Follow-up to rekordbox-unicode-paths.patch, which only covered the case where
 the PDB and the filesystem disagree about Unicode *normalisation*. The field
 report that prompted it turned out to be the other case: a FAT stick mounted
 without utf8/iocharset=utf8 hands the accented character back in the kernel's
 default charset, so no normalisation of the PDB name will ever equal it and
 the load still did nothing.
 .
 pi/usb-mount.sh fixes that at the mount, but a stick mounted by anything else
 (an older usb-mount.sh, a desktop automounter, a hand-typed mount) still lands
 here, and "the track will not load" is a miserable thing to debug from behind
 a pair of decks. So when the exact path and both normalisations miss, the
 directory is listed and matched on its "ASCII skeleton": the name with every
 non-ASCII character removed. Every mangling of an accent leaves the skeleton
 identical, so the file is found whatever the mount did to its name, while two
 files whose skeletons collide (names differing only in accented characters)
 are refused rather than guessed at.
 .
 The listing is cached per directory and thread_local, so a whole stick in the
 wrong charset costs one readdir per folder rather than one per track, and the
 concurrent per-device parse threads stay out of each other's way without a
 lock. A successful match is logged as a warning naming the mount fix, since
 finding the file this way means the stick is still mounted wrongly.
Author: Pioneered CI <pioneered-build@localhost>
Forwarded: no

